===Etymology 1===
{{PIE word|en|tréyes}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*peh₂ǵ-}}
{{rfc|en|Middle Scots comes after Middle English.}}
From {{inh|en|enm|travelen|t=to make a laborious journey, travel}} from {{der|en|gmw-msc|travailen|t=to toil, work, travel}}, alteration of {{cog|enm|travaillen|t=to toil, work}}, from {{der|en|fro|travailler|t=to trouble, suffer, be worn out}}. See the {{glossary|doublet}} {{doublet|en|travail|notext=1}}.

Largely displaced  {{m|en|fare}}, from {{cog|ang|faran|t=to go [a long distance], to travel}}. More at {{m|en|fare}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|++|pres_ptc2=traveling|pres_ptc2_qual=US|past2=traveled|past2_qual=US}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be on a [[journey]], often for pleasure or business and with luggage; to go from one place to another.
#: {{ux|en|John seems to spend as much time '''travelling''' as he does in the office.}}
#* '''1661''', John Stephens, ''An Historical Discourse...'', Prol.:
#*: He that [[fears|feareth]] [[oblatration]] must not '''travel'''.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1930|tlr=w:en:Marmaduke Pickthall|title=w:en:The Meaning of the Glorious Koran|section=surah 28, verse 29|url=|page=|text=Then, when Moses had fulfilled the term, and was '''travelling''' with his housefolk, he saw in the distance a fire and said unto his housefolk: Bide ye (here). Lo! I see in the distance a fire; peradventure I shall bring you tidings thence, or a brand from the fire that ye may warm yourselves.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[pass]] from one place to another; to move or [[transmit]].
#: {{ux|en|Soundwaves can '''travel''' through water.}}
#: {{ux|en|The supposedly secret news of Mary's engagement '''travelled''' quickly through her group of friends.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|basketball}} To move illegally by walking or running without dribbling the ball.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To travel throughout (a place).
#: {{ux|en|I’ve '''travelled''' the world.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To force to journey.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Ireland|passage=They shall not be '''travailed''' forth of their own franchises.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[labour]]; to [[travail]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1707| author=Richard Baxter | title=The Practical Works of the Late Reverend and Pious Mr. Richard Baxter |page=646| passage=''Necessity'' will make men fare hard, and work hard, and '''travel''' hard, go bare, and suffer much; yea it will even cut off a leg or arm to save their lives;}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1719| title=The Acceptable Sacrifice|author=William Tilly| page=335| passage=We labour sore, and '''travel''' hard, and much Study is a Weariness to our Flesh; and of making many Books there is no End.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1794| chapter=Resignation| author=| title=A Complete Edition of the Poets of Great Britain.Volume 10|editor=| page=144| passage=Man holds in constant service bound The blustering winds and seas; Nor suns disdain to '''travel''' hard Their master, man, to please;}}

